WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.250
&lt;i&gt;Against The Ice &lt;/i&gt;is
the tense historical tale

00:00:03.333 --> 00:00:05.000
of two arctic explorers,

00:00:05.083 --> 00:00:07.417
Ejnar Mikkelsen
and Iver Iversen,

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.708
who must survive
the sub-zero conditions

00:00:09.792 --> 00:00:10.667
of Greenland

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:13.375
as they seek discoveries of
an earlier expedition.

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:16.458
Based on the book
&lt;i&gt;Two Against The Ice&lt;/i&gt;

00:00:16.542 --> 00:00:17.667
by Ejnar Mikkelsen himself,

00:00:18.667 --> 00:00:20.083
this remarkable story
of survival

00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:21.958
in the early 20th century

00:00:22.042 --> 00:00:24.250
might seem like it was
dreamt up for a movie.

00:00:24.333 --> 00:00:27.917
In fact, director Peter
Flinth first read the book

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.083
when it was given to him by

00:00:29.167 --> 00:00:31.375
Queen Margrethe II
of Denmark,

00:00:31.458 --> 00:00:33.292
who thought it would make
a beautiful film.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:36.250
But it is an account
of real history

00:00:36.333 --> 00:00:38.833
and the resulting drama
sticks close to the events

00:00:38.917 --> 00:00:41.167
of Mikkelsen and Iversen's
journey as they happened.

00:00:41.833 --> 00:00:44.958
Here's the true story behind
&lt;i&gt;Against The Ice&lt;/i&gt;.

00:00:47.167 --> 00:00:49.750
Let's start with
the Denmark Expedition,

00:00:49.833 --> 00:00:51.417
which is mentioned
throughout the film

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.625
as the reason that
Mikkelsen and Iversen

00:00:53.708 --> 00:00:55.542
make
their initial excursion.

00:00:55.625 --> 00:00:57.042
This is why we're here,
boys.

00:00:57.625 --> 00:00:58.583
Without these findings,

00:00:58.667 --> 00:01:00.125
the north of Greenland
could be lost,

00:01:00.208 --> 00:01:01.333
claimed by the Americans.

00:01:01.417 --> 00:01:02.583
In the events that happened

00:01:02.667 --> 00:01:04.208
before &lt;i&gt;Against The Ice&lt;/i&gt;
begins,

00:01:04.292 --> 00:01:05.958
the Denmark Expedition saw
a crew

00:01:06.042 --> 00:01:07.917
led by
Ludvig Mylius-Erichsen

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.792
set out to map
the last parts

00:01:09.875 --> 00:01:11.000
of northeast Greenland.

00:01:11.083 --> 00:01:12.542
At the time
of their journey,

00:01:12.625 --> 00:01:15.583
which took place
between 1906 and 1908,

00:01:15.667 --> 00:01:18.417
it was believed that a lake
called the Peary Channel

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:19.833
cut Greenland in two.

00:01:20.458 --> 00:01:22.542
Mylius-Erichsen formed
a smaller team,

00:01:22.625 --> 00:01:25.583
composed of cartographer
Niels Peter Høeg Hagen

00:01:25.667 --> 00:01:28.458
and native Greenlander
Jørgen Brønlund,

00:01:28.542 --> 00:01:29.875
to investigate
whether or not

00:01:29.958 --> 00:01:32.000
the Peary Channel
actually existed.

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:34.875
Why don't any of you want to
go with them?

00:01:34.958 --> 00:01:36.125
You saw the map.

00:01:36.208 --> 00:01:37.875
I mean, it's like walking
from Moscow to Rome

00:01:37.958 --> 00:01:39.542
looking for
a stack of stones.

00:01:39.625 --> 00:01:41.042
During that journey,

00:01:41.125 --> 00:01:43.458
the trio ran into
various hardships,

00:01:43.542 --> 00:01:45.458
such as mild weather
melting the ice

00:01:45.542 --> 00:01:47.417
they would need to cross
to get back to their ship

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:49.292
and, eventually,
all three succumbed to

00:01:49.375 --> 00:01:51.292
the extreme conditions
they faced.

00:01:52.917 --> 00:01:54.042
Brønlund's body
was discovered

00:01:54.125 --> 00:01:55.417
in a makeshift cave,

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.417
which the film alludes to
in one scene,

00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:58.375
while the bodies of

00:01:58.458 --> 00:01:59.917
Mylius-Erichsen
and Høeg Hagen

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.000
were never found.

00:02:01.083 --> 00:02:02.708
Anyone would think
you can't make it.

00:02:04.333 --> 00:02:06.500
You always believe
there'll be a way.

00:02:06.583 --> 00:02:08.875
However, the
Denmark Expedition proved

00:02:08.958 --> 00:02:10.792
the Peary Channel
didn't exist

00:02:10.875 --> 00:02:13.583
and left information of
their discovery in a can.

00:02:14.125 --> 00:02:15.917
As we hear early on
in the film…

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.208
What's a can?

00:02:17.292 --> 00:02:20.000
A can's a stack of stones
you can see from far away.

00:02:20.583 --> 00:02:22.667
Explorers often used these
to store messages

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:24.333
and reports for others.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:25.875
More importantly,

00:02:25.958 --> 00:02:27.750
it provided a way
for the discovery

00:02:27.833 --> 00:02:29.708
to be passed on
to other explorers.

00:02:30.667 --> 00:02:33.292
This is where the heroes of
&lt;i&gt;Against The Ice &lt;/i&gt;come in:

00:02:33.375 --> 00:02:35.375
the mystery of
the Denmark Expedition's

00:02:35.458 --> 00:02:37.208
fate-gripped explorers
of the time,

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:39.000
including Ejnar Mikkelsen,

00:02:39.083 --> 00:02:41.625
played in the film by
Nikolaj Coster-Waldau.

00:02:41.708 --> 00:02:43.667
Mikkelsen was commissioned
to lead a search

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:46.125
to find Mylius-Erichsen
and his research.

00:02:46.667 --> 00:02:48.250
He put together
a small crew,

00:02:48.333 --> 00:02:49.667
seen briefly in the film,

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:51.708
and set off in the ship
&lt;i&gt;The Alabama&lt;/i&gt;

00:02:51.792 --> 00:02:54.125
on the 20th of June 1909.

00:02:55.333 --> 00:02:57.458
However, what we don't see
in the film is

00:02:57.542 --> 00:03:00.083
how Mikkelsen came to be
commissioned for this trip.

00:03:00.167 --> 00:03:02.250
The sailor was
originally approached by

00:03:02.333 --> 00:03:03.458
Lord Northcliffe,

00:03:03.542 --> 00:03:05.583
the owner of the &lt;i&gt;Daily Mail&lt;/i&gt;
at the time,

00:03:05.667 --> 00:03:07.292
and proposed
that he fund a trip

00:03:07.375 --> 00:03:09.958
for Mikkelsen to find
the dead men's papers.

00:03:10.042 --> 00:03:10.917
In return,

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:13.583
anything he found would be
published in the newspaper.

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.167
Mikkelsen liked
the sound of the offer,

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:18.417
however revealed in his book
that he felt:

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:20.708
"As a Dane,
I did not like the idea of

00:03:20.792 --> 00:03:22.750
an Englishman paying for
the expedition

00:03:22.833 --> 00:03:24.667
and of his money acquiring
the rights to

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:27.125
what three Danes had given
their lives to achieve".

00:03:28.417 --> 00:03:29.958
Mikkelsen went
back to Denmark

00:03:30.042 --> 00:03:31.083
and approached the committee

00:03:31.167 --> 00:03:33.167
who first commissioned
the Denmark Expedition

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:36.208
about leading a mission to
retrieve the missing papers.

00:03:36.292 --> 00:03:38.417
They agreed to pay
half the cost of the trip,

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:39.958
with Mikkelsen funding
the rest.

00:03:41.208 --> 00:03:42.375
The crew travelled to

00:03:42.458 --> 00:03:44.125
where Brønlund's body
was discovered

00:03:44.208 --> 00:03:46.125
and, in the spring of 1910,

00:03:46.208 --> 00:03:48.792
Mikkelsen and the crew's
engineer, Iver Iversen,

00:03:48.875 --> 00:03:50.292
played by Joe Cole,

00:03:50.375 --> 00:03:52.333
set out to see
if they could find the can

00:03:52.417 --> 00:03:54.042
that Brønlund's notes
alluded to.

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:57.042
The perilous journey to find
the can is based on

00:03:57.125 --> 00:03:59.833
the first-hand accounts
Mikkelsen wrote in his book,

00:03:59.917 --> 00:04:01.417
as is
the even harder journey

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:02.542
they had to make back.

00:04:03.458 --> 00:04:05.833
This is what happened to
Mylius-Erichsen, isn't it?

00:04:06.417 --> 00:04:08.417
Another part of
the return journey

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:11.833
that the film alludes to is
the men's struggle for food.

00:04:11.917 --> 00:04:13.625
On my calculations,

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:16.042
I've lost us

00:04:16.125 --> 00:04:17.917
two weeks' worth
of dog food.

00:04:19.042 --> 00:04:20.458
I've lost us half our tea

00:04:20.542 --> 00:04:22.833
and one can of paraffin.

00:04:24.792 --> 00:04:26.792
While we see the pair
having to rely on eating

00:04:26.875 --> 00:04:28.625
some rather foul things,

00:04:28.708 --> 00:04:31.083
the book reveals that
they even resorted to

00:04:31.167 --> 00:04:32.042
eating mould.

00:04:32.125 --> 00:04:33.708
As Iversen is claimed
to have said:

00:04:33.792 --> 00:04:35.250
"You said
we need vegetables!

00:04:35.333 --> 00:04:37.542
Isn't mould also
a kind of vegetable?"

00:04:38.417 --> 00:04:40.167
To be clear, it is not.

00:04:41.167 --> 00:04:43.000
Despite having to trek back
on foot

00:04:43.083 --> 00:04:44.042
and without supplies,

00:04:44.125 --> 00:04:46.292
their ordeal was
far from over.

00:04:46.375 --> 00:04:49.208
When they arrived back at
their camp in November 1910,

00:04:49.292 --> 00:04:51.417
they discovered that
the crew had abandoned them,

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:53.042
just like in the film,

00:04:53.125 --> 00:04:55.125
leaving them only with
a small wooden hut

00:04:55.208 --> 00:04:56.958
made from their ship,
&lt;i&gt;The Alabama&lt;/i&gt;,

00:04:57.042 --> 00:04:59.375
hence why it's dubbed
Alabama Cottage in the film

00:04:59.458 --> 00:05:01.250
and Alabama Hudson
in real life,

00:05:01.333 --> 00:05:03.167
and supplies to last them
through the winter.

00:05:04.042 --> 00:05:05.208
They left us!

00:05:05.917 --> 00:05:07.083
No!

00:05:07.167 --> 00:05:08.333
No!

00:05:10.708 --> 00:05:12.708
Amazingly, Mikkelsen
and Iversen survived

00:05:12.792 --> 00:05:14.000
in this small cabin,

00:05:14.083 --> 00:05:16.042
having to fend off
physical dangers,

00:05:16.125 --> 00:05:17.875
such as
an attack from a bear,

00:05:18.417 --> 00:05:19.542
as well as
the mental effects

00:05:19.625 --> 00:05:21.375
of such extreme isolation.

00:05:21.458 --> 00:05:23.292
I met my grandfather
earlier.

00:05:24.083 --> 00:05:25.167
Your grandfather?

00:05:25.917 --> 00:05:26.875
Here?

00:05:27.792 --> 00:05:29.875
No, just up on the hill.

00:05:29.958 --> 00:05:31.458
Where the film differs from
reality

00:05:31.542 --> 00:05:35.208
is that their ordeal was
even harder in real life.

00:05:35.292 --> 00:05:36.167
Take, for instance,

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:38.458
the bear attack that occurs
late in the film.

00:05:38.542 --> 00:05:40.625
While the movie shows
Mikkelsen and Iversen

00:05:40.708 --> 00:05:43.167
scaring off the bear
by making loud noises,

00:05:43.250 --> 00:05:44.875
in Mikkelsen's book
he recounts

00:05:44.958 --> 00:05:46.458
how this attack saw
the bear actually

00:05:46.542 --> 00:05:48.250
make its way into the hut,

00:05:48.333 --> 00:05:50.458
kicking up steam
from a pan of water

00:05:50.542 --> 00:05:51.917
that had been boiling
on the stove

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:54.000
and Iversen having to
shoot it to death.

00:05:55.750 --> 00:05:56.917
In a stroke of luck,

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:58.542
something that,
up until that point,

00:05:58.625 --> 00:06:00.500
had been in short supply
for both of them,

00:06:00.583 --> 00:06:02.833
they were discovered
by a passing Norwegian ship

00:06:02.917 --> 00:06:05.125
on July 21st 1912.

00:06:05.917 --> 00:06:08.667
They had been separated from
any other human contact

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:11.083
for almost 28 months.

00:06:11.708 --> 00:06:13.292
Despite the hardships
they endured,

00:06:13.375 --> 00:06:15.417
some elements
of their journey live on,

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:17.167
such as the Alabama Cottage.

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:19.667
The hut in which Mikkelsen
and Iversen survived

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:20.833
is still standing

00:06:20.917 --> 00:06:23.292
and, in 2016,
was completely restored

00:06:23.375 --> 00:06:25.875
by the northeast Greenland
company Nanok

00:06:25.958 --> 00:06:27.708
for
the North Greenland Museum.

00:06:28.667 --> 00:06:30.167
Bringing Mikkelsen's story
to life

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:32.917
has also been a passion
project for Coster-Waldau,

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:34.333
who was sent
a copy of the book

00:06:34.417 --> 00:06:37.042
by his close friend
Peter Flinth in 2011.

00:06:37.667 --> 00:06:39.542
He said about
making the film:

00:06:39.625 --> 00:06:41.500
"It combines a lot of themes
that excite me:

00:06:41.583 --> 00:06:43.625
adventure, Greenland,
companionship,

00:06:43.708 --> 00:06:45.000
loyalty and love".

00:06:45.542 --> 00:06:46.833
The actor also has

00:06:46.917 --> 00:06:48.292
a personal connection
to Greenland:

00:06:48.375 --> 00:06:50.208
his wife,
actor and singer Nukaaka,

00:06:50.292 --> 00:06:51.375
is from the country.

00:06:52.625 --> 00:06:55.125
You can watch how Mikkelsen
and Iversen's story unfolds

00:06:55.208 --> 00:06:57.583
in &lt;i&gt;Against The Ice &lt;/i&gt;streaming
on Netflix now.

00:06:58.167 --> 00:06:59.625
And, for more true stories
behind

00:06:59.708 --> 00:07:01.042
your favourite shows
and films,

00:07:01.125 --> 00:07:03.292
why not subscribe to
Still Watching Netflix?

